From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 21:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] py/scheduler: Run scheduled callbacks on the main thread
 only, with a GIL too.

Without a GIL the scheduler already runs only on the main thread. With one
(the esp32 port) it runs on whichever thread calls mp_handle_pending()
next, and a thread that waits in a socket call does that every time its
wait comes round. So a soft timer's callback, LVGL's timer pass, the
WebREPL poll and anything given to micropython.schedule() run there,
nested inside that thread's code and on its stack, which is often small.

On an ESP32-P4 with a thread making HTTPS requests for 25 s, a 5 ms
multimer timer ran on that thread for 713 to 1592 of 3720 to 4896 ticks,
an LVGL timer for 285 to 637 of 1588 to 2175, and a callback scheduled
when the thread's job ended ran on the thread itself every time. A thread
that only sleeps took 2060 of 4974 ticks. An app that drew from its timer
there stopped the board with no traceback.

Run the scheduler on the main thread only, as without a GIL, and let a
thread that isn't the main one hand the GIL on at its next bounce while
callbacks are pending, since it can't run them itself. Without that a
thread in a pure Python loop would keep the GIL, and the callbacks
waiting, until it blocked.

With the patch, in five runs of the same test and one with a sleeping
thread, not one callback ran off the main thread. The main thread ran
more ticks than before: 3587 against 2983 with about 20 requests, 3013
against 2128 with about 30. The cost is that a tick now waits for the
main thread to get the GIL back: time between ticks over 20 ms grew from
about 170 ms per request to about 360 ms, most of it in the TLS
handshake. Those ticks used to run on the requesting thread instead.
---
diff --git a/py/scheduler.c b/py/scheduler.c
--- a/py/scheduler.c
+++ b/py/scheduler.c
@@ -283,9 +283,11 @@
     // Handle any pending callbacks.
     #if MICROPY_ENABLE_SCHEDULER
     bool run_scheduler = (MP_STATE_VM(sched_state) == MP_SCHED_PENDING);
-    #if MICROPY_PY_THREAD && !MICROPY_PY_THREAD_GIL
-    // Avoid races by running the scheduler on the main thread, only.
-    // (Not needed if GIL enabled, as GIL ensures thread safety here.)
+    #if MICROPY_PY_THREAD
+    // Run the scheduler on the main thread, only. Without a GIL that avoids
+    // races. With one it keeps a callback off another thread's stack: a
+    // thread waiting in a socket call would otherwise run timer callbacks,
+    // the REPL and anything else scheduled, nested inside its own code.
     run_scheduler = run_scheduler && mp_thread_is_main_thread();
     #endif
     if (run_scheduler) {
@@ -300,7 +302,7 @@
 // nothing would wake the wait until some unrelated interrupt.
 static inline bool mp_event_callback_ready(void) {
     #if MICROPY_ENABLE_SCHEDULER
-    #if MICROPY_PY_THREAD && !MICROPY_PY_THREAD_GIL
+    #if MICROPY_PY_THREAD
     if (!mp_thread_is_main_thread()) {
         return false;
     }
diff --git a/py/vm.c b/py/vm.c
--- a/py/vm.c
+++ b/py/vm.c
@@ -1381,8 +1381,11 @@
                     gil_divisor = MICROPY_PY_THREAD_GIL_VM_DIVISOR;
                     #endif
                     #if MICROPY_ENABLE_SCHEDULER
-                    // can only switch threads if the scheduler is unlocked
-                    if (MP_STATE_VM(sched_state) == MP_SCHED_IDLE)
+                    // can only switch threads if the scheduler is unlocked;
+                    // another thread also lets go while callbacks are pending,
+                    // since only the main thread runs them
+                    if (MP_STATE_VM(sched_state) == MP_SCHED_IDLE
+                        || (MP_STATE_VM(sched_state) == MP_SCHED_PENDING && !mp_thread_is_main_thread()))
                     #endif
                     {
                     MP_THREAD_GIL_EXIT();
-- 
